=================================================
Lab 1: File Path Traversal, Simple Case
=================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website shows product images. Each image is loaded through a URL that includes the filename.
The server takes that filename and reads the file from its image folder without checking where the path actually points.
We can use ../ to leave that folder and read other files on the server.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<name>.
The server joins the filename to its image folder, /var/www/images/, and returns that file's contents.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The filename is used directly in the file path, with no check for path separators or ../ sequences.
That means the request can point to any file the web server process is allowed to read.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Directory traversal (../): This moves up one folder. Each ../ removes one level from the current path.
Starting folder: The images folder is /var/www/images/. Three ../ moves up to the root folder /.
Root-level file: /etc/passwd is a Linux file that lists user accounts. It is readable by default, so it makes a clear proof that traversal works.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a normal filename, we send:

../../../etc/passwd

The server builds a path that roughly looks like:

/var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd

The three ../ take us from /var/www/images/ up to /, and then the path continues to /etc/passwd.
The server reads the file and returns its contents as the response.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Find the filename in the image URL and replace it with ../../../etc/passwd.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the target as /image.
* It sends a GET request with filename set to ../../../etc/passwd. The params argument adds it to the URL query string.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The server trusts the filename it receives and builds the file path by joining it to a fixed folder.
Because ../ is a normal part of a path, the server follows it out of the image folder and reads any file it can access.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths directly from user input.
Resolve the full path and check that it still starts with the allowed folder before reading the file.
Use an allowlist of known image names, or map user input to a file ID the server controls.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files out of paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A ../ sequence moves up one folder, so user input can escape the intended directory.
Always check the resolved path, not just the raw input.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
